{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgsyrD6w3rsUHwAoMhSiE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The crude string check shows the reference answer somewhere in the pre-loop text for 59% of cut samples, which overstates real hits since short answers match by accident. Still, the model often makes progress before looping. Finished samples have a median length of about 5,600 tokens, the same scale as the median loop onset (6,500), so looping is a hazard that grows with length. Next I'll audit the weights for anomalies across layers and heads. That means norm-weight statistics, per-head q/k/v/o norms in the full-attention layers, and DeltaNet decay/gate parameters. An outlier (one head or norm much larger than its peers) would be a prime suspect for over-strong copying; a smooth profile would refute the \"single tampered component\" idea and point me to activation-based localization instead.\n\n```python\n# Hypothesis B: an attention component (head/norm) has anomalous weights. First list shapes and norm stats per layer.\nP='model.language_model.layers.'\nshp={}\nfor n in NAMES:\n    if n.startswith(P+'3.') or n.startswith(P+'0.'):\n        shp[n.replace(P,'')]=tuple(load(n).shape)\nprint(shp)\nrows=[]\nfor L in range(24):\n    for nm in ['input_layernorm.weight','post_attention_layernorm.weight']:\n        w=load(P+f'{L}.{nm}'); rows.append(dict(L=L, t=nm.split('.')[0][:10], mean=w.mean().item(), std=w.std().item(), mx=w.abs().max().item()))\ndf=pd.DataFrame(rows).pivot(index='L', columns='t', values=['mean','std','mx']).round(3)\nprint(df.to_string())\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1553,"cache_read_input_tokens":27499,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1553},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_tr